1
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:15,165
JAMIE: Levi Strauss just raised profit guidance on tariff refunds while their actual sales outlook is less optimistic—so they're basically a Treasury bond with a leather patch now.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/07/levi-strauss-levi-q3-2026-earnings.html

2
00:00:15,165 --> 00:00:30,352
ALEX: So yesterday we watched chip stocks rally on AI demand, but Cisco just reminded us that the real margin lives upstream—they're up 4 point five four percent on pure infrastructure play, which means while everyone's betting on the silicon, the networking vendors are the ones actually charging tolls on the highway.

3
00:00:30,352 --> 00:00:44,954
ALEX: So the tariff refund itself is the thing—Levi's got hit with duties on imported goods, sat on that liability for a year, and now they're reversing it on the books. That's a one-time accounting gift, not a single pair of jeans sold. The guidance hike is entirely that reversal.

4
00:00:44,954 --> 00:00:58,097
MAYA: Wait, so when they say profit guidance is up, they're not saying the business got better—they're saying the government's tariff math worked in their favor and they get to recognize that now. That's like celebrating because your landlord finally applied a security deposit credit.

5
00:00:58,097 --> 00:01:06,858
JAMIE: And the part that should terrify investors is buried in the release—they're actually guiding down on revenue next quarter. So tariffs saved the earnings line while demand is collapsing.

6
00:01:06,858 --> 00:01:23,797
ALEX: Right. The tariff reversal is masking what's actually happening in the wholesale channel. Retailers are ordering less, inventory is still bloated from last year, and Levi's is trapped between raising prices to protect margins—which kills volume—or holding price and watching margins compress. The refund just bought them time to not answer that question in this earnings call.

7
00:01:23,797 --> 00:01:33,434
ALEX: Fed's minutes show zero appetite for a series of rate hikes—officials viewed that one hike as insurance, not the start of a campaign. Which means equities just got permission to keep grinding.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/fed-minutes-show-no-appetite-for-a-series-of-interest-rate-hikes-8b1c6436?mod=mw_rss_topstories

8
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:43,656
JAMIE: So they hiked once to say 'we're serious' and then immediately signaled they're done. That's the financial equivalent of a parent yelling at their kid and then sneaking them ice cream five minutes later.

9
00:01:43,656 --> 00:01:53,002
MAYA: The market heard 'no more hikes' and just decided inflation noise is background music now. Equities are going to keep ripping because the Fed basically admitted they're terrified of breaking something.

10
00:01:53,002 --> 00:02:02,055
ALEX: Constellation Brands is getting creative to bring back beer drinkers because overall demand is weakening. When your fix is creativity instead of a better product, you're not winning, you're stalling.

11
00:02:02,055 --> 00:02:10,525
JAMIE: They're still talking about 'creativity' language. That's how you know the underlying demand is broken—a company has to pretend innovation is happening instead of just selling more beer.

12
00:02:10,525 --> 00:02:24,251
MAYA: Anthropic's IPO is being called 'the most ridiculous of the year' by analysts, and I love that they're admitting it out loud. They have no model for what to pay for a company that burns cash on inference costs and has maybe one customer who matters.

13
00:02:24,251 --> 00:02:35,057
JAMIE: So the bearish advisory firm is basically saying 'we have no idea what this is worth, so sit this one out'—which is the most honest thing an analyst will say all year about an AI IPO.

14
00:02:35,057 --> 00:02:45,571
MAYA: Teenage Engineering's CEO just said they're stopping synth production because the economics don't work. When a boutique hardware maker admits niche manufacturing is dead, that's not noise—that's a quiet signal the whole category is broken.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/1007489/teengage-engineering-stop-making-synths

15
00:02:45,571 --> 00:02:58,421
MAYA: A supertanker just chartered for 76 million dollars to haul oil from the Gulf to China—that's ten times pre-war rates. Someone paid more to move one shipment than the annual salary of a thousand people combined because shipping routes are now severely disrupted.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/07/supertanker-from-us-to-china-chartered-for-76-million-source.html

16
00:02:58,421 --> 00:03:16,236
ALEX: The tanker shortage is real—vessels are getting rerouted around the Cape of Good Hope instead of the Suez, adding weeks to transit time. So you're not just paying more per ton, you're also burning fuel for an extra two weeks at sea. The math on that charter says someone needed that cargo badly enough to absorb both margins getting eviscerated.

17
00:03:16,236 --> 00:03:22,077
MAYA: Levi's just proved that when the government hands you money, call it earnings and hope nobody checks the closet.

18
00:03:22,077 --> 00:03:27,918
ALEX: Next quarter they'll be back asking for sympathy when that credit runs out and the jeans still aren't selling.

19
00:03:27,918 --> 00:03:32,006
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
